<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virunum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virunum was the capital of the Roman province of Noricum, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollfeld">Zollfeld</a> plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. It was founded under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> around 45 CE to replace the hilltop settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalensberg">Magdalensberg</a>, and had a forum, temples and an amphitheatre. It declined in the late Roman period and was abandoned during the migrations of the 5th and 6th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virunum.jpg" alt="Virunum" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Jaritz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maria_Saal_Zollfeld_Virunum_Arena_Suedteil_22092006_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Membership lists, such as the graffiti of Dura-Europos and the bronze album of <b>Virunum</b>, name only men, and Gordon concludes that the cult was for men only, though Porphyry refers to female initiates and David Jonathan has suggested that women took part in some places.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 16 BCE Noricum was absorbed peacefully into the Roman Empire, becoming the province of Noricum with towns such as <b>Virunum</b> and Celeia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-noricum">Kingdom of Noricum</a></li></ul></section>
